-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Concurrency Policy: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+**Decision**: Each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and displays pending competing revisions. Review-export needs a revision comparison workflow and excludes unresolved visits from its export set.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+**Rationale**: A bigger review queue is preferred over maintaining merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. The team accepts higher coordinator workload to avoid silent semantic combination.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Decisions & Alternatives
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | Bigger review queue accepted over merge rules that miss scientific relationships |
+
+## Submission Envelope
+
+A submission carries:
+- `visit_id` (UUID) — the visit being edited
+- `base_revision` (integer) — the revision number the edit started from
+- `fields` (object) — the complete visit fields: `observation_date`, `observers` (string[]), `species` (array of `{species_id, cover_percent}`), `notes` (string)
+- `idempotency_key` (string) — client-generated, unique per submission attempt
+
+The server accepts a submission if:
+- The `visit_id` exists (or is new)
+- The `base_revision` matches the current head revision of that visit
+- The `idempotency_key` is unused (or matches an identical prior payload)
+
+On acceptance, the server assigns the next revision number (`base_revision + 1`), stores the submission as a new revision, and returns the assigned revision number.
+
+If `base_revision` does not match the current head, the submission is rejected with the current head revision and its full fields, so the tablet can display the competing revision and let the worker rebase.
+
+## Current-State Retrieval
+
+`GET /visits/{visit_id}` returns:
+- `head_revision` (integer) — latest accepted revision number
+- `head_fields` (object) — fields of the head revision
+- `unresolved` (boolean) — true if there are other revisions with the same `base_revision` as the head (i.e., competing revisions exist)
+- `competing_revisions` (array) — if `unresolved`, the other revisions sharing the head's `base_revision`, each with `revision`, `fields`, `author_id`, `submitted_at`
+
+This lets a caller distinguish a clean head from one with unresolved review work.
+
+## Unresolved-Work Representation
+
+A visit has unresolved review work when multiple revisions share the same `base_revision` (i.e., they were concurrent edits from the same starting state). The set of competing revisions includes the head and any siblings.
+
+The head revision is the one with the highest revision number among the competitors — it represents the most recent arrival but carries no inherent authority.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator resolves a set of competing revisions by selecting one as the new head and optionally producing a merged field set.
+
+`POST /visits/{visit_id}/resolve` carries:
+- `selected_revision` (integer) — one of the competing revision numbers
+- `merged_fields` (object, optional) — if provided, becomes the field set of a new resolution revision; if omitted, the selected revision's fields become the new head
+
+On acceptance, the server creates a new revision (`head_revision + 1`) with:
+- `fields` = `merged_fields` or the selected revision's fields
+- `author_id` = coordinator ID
+- `resolved_from` = array of the competing revision numbers
+- `resolution_type` = "selected" or "merged"
+
+The new revision becomes the head. The competing revisions remain stored with their original content. `unresolved` becomes false for the visit.
 
